WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.565 --> 00:00:03.397
- Dreams without goals are just dreams.

00:00:03.397 --> 00:00:04.351
I just can't live my life

00:00:04.351 --> 00:00:06.348
based upon what other
people think about me.

00:00:06.348 --> 00:00:07.881
Something you don't feel good about,

00:00:07.881 --> 00:00:09.214
then don't do it.

00:00:09.214 --> 00:00:10.551
How many ghosts are going
to be around your bed

00:00:10.551 --> 00:00:11.898
when your time comes?

00:00:11.898 --> 00:00:14.461
You'll never see a U-Haul behind a hearse.

00:00:14.461 --> 00:00:16.553
I got there just by workin' hard.

00:00:16.553 --> 00:00:18.399
To make sure you have
something to fall back on.

00:00:18.399 --> 00:00:20.172
There is no one discipline,

00:00:20.172 --> 00:00:22.089
there is no one gospel.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.725
Well, I will hand you the cup, my dear,

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:28.080
the cup will be there to be handed to you.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.719
I wanted to say thank you.

00:00:29.719 --> 00:00:30.878
- [Woman] A-ha.

00:00:30.878 --> 00:00:31.840
- It was my way of saying thank you.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:32.673
(whooshing)

00:00:32.673 --> 00:00:35.117
- He's and American actor and filmmaker.

00:00:35.117 --> 00:00:37.613
He's receiver two Golden
Globe awards, a Tony award,

00:00:37.613 --> 00:00:39.436
and two Academy awards.

00:00:39.436 --> 00:00:42.874
In 2015, he received a
Lifetime Achievement Award

00:00:42.874 --> 00:00:45.416
at the 73rd Golden Globes.

00:00:45.416 --> 00:00:47.576
He's Denzel Washington, and here are his

00:00:47.576 --> 00:00:48.930
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.714
(whooshing)

00:00:51.714 --> 00:00:55.777
- Dreams without goals are just dreams.

00:00:55.777 --> 00:00:59.330
And they ultimately fuel disappointment.

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.566
Goals on the road to achievement

00:01:02.566 --> 00:01:05.634
cannot be achieved without discipline

00:01:05.634 --> 00:01:06.967
and consistency.

00:01:09.038 --> 00:01:12.571
I pray that you all put
your shoes way under the bed

00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:14.905
at night so that you
got to get on your knees

00:01:14.905 --> 00:01:16.669
in the morning to find them.

00:01:16.669 --> 00:01:18.594
(crowd cheering)
(crowd applauding)

00:01:18.594 --> 00:01:21.091
Anything you want, good.

00:01:21.091 --> 00:01:22.174
You can have.

00:01:23.586 --> 00:01:24.586
So claim it.

00:01:26.213 --> 00:01:28.451
Work hard to get it.

00:01:28.451 --> 00:01:32.618
When you get it, reach
back, pull someone else up.

00:01:33.458 --> 00:01:37.635
Each one, teach one.
(audience applauding)

00:01:37.635 --> 00:01:40.552
Don't just aspire to make a living.

00:01:43.068 --> 00:01:45.401
Aspire to make a difference.

00:01:47.354 --> 00:01:48.458
- [Interviewer] You don't
pay a lot of attention

00:01:48.458 --> 00:01:50.397
to reviews and those kind of things?

00:01:50.397 --> 00:01:53.306
- Well, you know, you're
always affected by opinion,

00:01:53.306 --> 00:01:56.556
but the more opinions there are of you,

00:01:57.583 --> 00:02:01.166
of me, the more I, the
less I look at them,

00:02:02.552 --> 00:02:04.201
because I just can't live my life

00:02:04.201 --> 00:02:06.506
based upon what other
people think about me.

00:02:06.506 --> 00:02:08.230
So I can't concern myself too much

00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:09.832
with what other people think, you know?

00:02:09.832 --> 00:02:10.875
It's just not healthy.

00:02:10.875 --> 00:02:13.786
I don't think I could
continue to do what I do

00:02:13.786 --> 00:02:16.155
if I was constantly worrying
about what somebody thought

00:02:16.155 --> 00:02:17.155
about it.

00:02:17.155 --> 00:02:19.002
You don't have to compromise yourself.

00:02:19.002 --> 00:02:20.002
You know?

00:02:20.002 --> 00:02:21.627
It's something you don't feel good about,

00:02:21.627 --> 00:02:22.810
then don't it.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:24.154
The most important choices I've made

00:02:24.154 --> 00:02:25.321
was to say no.

00:02:26.476 --> 00:02:29.066
And I've said no many a time,

00:02:29.066 --> 00:02:32.282
to films that I just
didn't, especially early on.

00:02:32.282 --> 00:02:34.375
That I just didn't feel comfortable with.

00:02:34.375 --> 00:02:37.652
There was one script
that was brought to me,

00:02:37.652 --> 00:02:39.762
I called it the nigger they couldn't kill.

00:02:39.762 --> 00:02:42.478
(audience laughing)

00:02:42.478 --> 00:02:44.144
'Cause they were going to like,

00:02:44.144 --> 00:02:47.226
he was accused of raping
his wife, you know,

00:02:47.226 --> 00:02:49.630
in the '40s, and they tried to hang him

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.215
but he didn't die.

00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:53.101
(audience laughing)

00:02:53.101 --> 00:02:54.715
And they tried to electrocute him,

00:02:54.715 --> 00:02:56.258
and they were like, it's a comedy!

00:02:56.258 --> 00:02:57.873
I'm like, it isn't funny to me.

00:02:57.873 --> 00:02:59.324
(audience laughing)
Not funny to me.

00:02:59.324 --> 00:03:02.945
So, I actually called Cyndi Porthia,

00:03:02.945 --> 00:03:06.162
who I was fortunate at
that time, and still,

00:03:06.162 --> 00:03:07.567
to be able to call.

00:03:07.567 --> 00:03:10.304
And I said, you know, this
movie's making, I'm sick.

00:03:10.304 --> 00:03:13.442
And they offered me $600,000

00:03:13.442 --> 00:03:15.508
to play the nigger they couldn't kill.

00:03:15.508 --> 00:03:17.505
(audience laughing)

00:03:17.505 --> 00:03:21.151
That's what I call it, (muffled speaking).

00:03:21.151 --> 00:03:24.156
He said to me, and I'll say this to you,

00:03:24.156 --> 00:03:27.734
he said, the first two, or
three, or four films you make

00:03:27.734 --> 00:03:29.812
in this industry, Denzel,
will determine how

00:03:29.812 --> 00:03:30.833
people see you.

00:03:30.833 --> 00:03:33.109
He said, I'm not going
to tell you what to do,

00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:37.428
but remember that the first
three, four, five films

00:03:37.428 --> 00:03:39.318
you make will determine
how you're perceived.

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:41.974
I turned it down, I turned the money down.

00:03:41.974 --> 00:03:43.174
I needed the money,

00:03:43.174 --> 00:03:44.742
I turned it down.

00:03:44.742 --> 00:03:46.391
Six months later I got Cry of Freedom.

00:03:46.391 --> 00:03:48.597
(audience cheering)
(audience applauding)

00:03:48.597 --> 00:03:50.103
(muffled speaking)

00:03:50.103 --> 00:03:51.671
So, stick to your guns.

00:03:51.671 --> 00:03:55.156
If you don't feel like you should do it,

00:03:55.156 --> 00:03:56.503
then don't do it.

00:03:56.503 --> 00:03:58.802
'Cause you don't want to be, you know,

00:03:58.802 --> 00:04:01.062
you don't want to be a
negative energy on the set,

00:04:01.062 --> 00:04:02.955
that's how I feel sometimes.

00:04:02.955 --> 00:04:04.070
I'm like, you know, I ain't doin' this

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:05.768
'cause I'm going to slap
somebody the second day.

00:04:05.768 --> 00:04:07.101
(audience laughing)

00:04:07.101 --> 00:04:08.694
So I just, you know.

00:04:08.694 --> 00:04:11.607
I think the most important
decisions you could make

00:04:11.607 --> 00:04:14.626
as an actor, actress, is saying no.

00:04:14.626 --> 00:04:16.066
Is saying no.

00:04:16.066 --> 00:04:18.330
You don't have to take your clothes off

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:20.327
if you don't want to, then do it.

00:04:20.327 --> 00:04:21.523
(audience applauding)

00:04:21.523 --> 00:04:22.660
To get something you never had,

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:25.910
you have to do something you never did.

00:04:26.886 --> 00:04:28.428
Les Brown's a motivational speaker,

00:04:28.428 --> 00:04:30.065
he made an analogy about this.

00:04:30.065 --> 00:04:32.200
He says, imagine you're on your deathbed,

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.187
and standing around your
deathbed are the ghosts

00:04:35.187 --> 00:04:38.147
representing your unfulfilled potential.

00:04:38.147 --> 00:04:40.664
The ghost of the ideas you never acted on,

00:04:40.664 --> 00:04:43.510
the ghost of the talents you didn't use,

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:45.251
and they're standing around your bed

00:04:45.251 --> 00:04:47.834
angry, disappointed, and upset.

00:04:49.027 --> 00:04:52.148
They say, we came to you
because you could have

00:04:52.148 --> 00:04:54.030
brought us to life, they say.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.234
And now we have to go
to the grave together.

00:04:57.234 --> 00:04:58.817
So I ask you today,

00:05:00.844 --> 00:05:02.477
how many ghosts are going
to be around your bed

00:05:02.477 --> 00:05:03.980
when your time comes?

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:08.062
You'll never see a U-Haul behind a hearse.

00:05:08.062 --> 00:05:10.888
I'll say it again,
you'll never see a U-Haul

00:05:10.888 --> 00:05:12.461
behind a hearse.

00:05:12.461 --> 00:05:13.949
Now I've been blessed to make hundreds

00:05:13.949 --> 00:05:16.891
of millions of dollars in my life,

00:05:16.891 --> 00:05:18.121
I can't take it with me.

00:05:18.121 --> 00:05:19.126
(grunting)

00:05:19.126 --> 00:05:20.301
And neither can you.

00:05:20.301 --> 00:05:22.461
So it's not how much you have,

00:05:22.461 --> 00:05:25.461
it's what you do with what you have.

00:05:27.548 --> 00:05:28.930
And we all have different gifts.

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:31.426
Some money, some love, some patience,

00:05:31.426 --> 00:05:34.765
some their ability to touch people,

00:05:34.765 --> 00:05:36.348
but we all have it.

00:05:37.626 --> 00:05:38.798
Use it.

00:05:38.798 --> 00:05:39.631
Share it.

00:05:40.605 --> 00:05:42.067
That's what counts.

00:05:42.067 --> 00:05:45.114
Not what you're driving,
not what you're flyin' in,

00:05:45.114 --> 00:05:46.805
not what kind of house
you bought your mama,

00:05:46.805 --> 00:05:47.947
but buy your mama a house.

00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:49.677
(crowd laughing)

00:05:49.677 --> 00:05:51.964
The one thing I'm the most happy about

00:05:51.964 --> 00:05:54.669
in terms of my career, is
the fact that I got there

00:05:54.669 --> 00:05:56.829
with the grace of God, first of all,

00:05:56.829 --> 00:06:00.556
but short of that, I got
there just by workin' hard.

00:06:00.556 --> 00:06:03.200
Not partyin' with the right people,

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:06.094
not compromising myself in any way

00:06:06.094 --> 00:06:07.555
or cutting any kind of deals.

00:06:07.555 --> 00:06:10.238
Just by working hard,
just by plugging along,

00:06:10.238 --> 00:06:12.962
sawing wood as I like to call it.

00:06:12.962 --> 00:06:14.018
(dramatic music)

00:06:14.018 --> 00:06:15.551
I'm a 20 year old sensation.

00:06:15.551 --> 00:06:19.951
I've found that nothing
in life is worthwhile

00:06:19.951 --> 00:06:22.163
unless you take risks.

00:06:22.163 --> 00:06:23.780
Nothing.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:27.923
Nelson Mandela said, there
is no passion to be found

00:06:27.923 --> 00:06:32.275
playing small and settling
for a life that's less

00:06:32.275 --> 00:06:36.054
than the one you're capable of living.

00:06:36.054 --> 00:06:37.888
Now I'm sure in your experiences in school

00:06:37.888 --> 00:06:40.256
and applying to college,
and picking your major,

00:06:40.256 --> 00:06:42.160
and deciding what you
want to do with life,

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.596
I'm sure people have told you to make sure

00:06:44.596 --> 00:06:46.398
you have something to fall back on.

00:06:46.398 --> 00:06:50.032
Make sure you got something
to fall back on, honey.

00:06:50.032 --> 00:06:51.954
But I never understood that concept,

00:06:51.954 --> 00:06:54.704
having something to fall back on.

00:06:55.582 --> 00:06:59.749
If I'm going to fall, I don't
want to fall back on anything

00:07:01.212 --> 00:07:03.325
except my faith.

00:07:03.325 --> 00:07:05.833
I want to fall forward.

00:07:05.833 --> 00:07:10.000
I figure at least this way
I'll see what I'm going to hit.

00:07:11.067 --> 00:07:12.150
Fall forward.

00:07:13.206 --> 00:07:14.873
This is what I mean.

00:07:15.957 --> 00:07:18.918
Reggie Jackson struck out
2,600 times in his career,

00:07:18.918 --> 00:07:21.387
the most in the history of baseball.

00:07:21.387 --> 00:07:22.874
But you don't hear about the strikeouts.

00:07:22.874 --> 00:07:25.374
People remember the home runs.

00:07:26.441 --> 00:07:27.524
Fall forward.

00:07:29.272 --> 00:07:32.664
Thomas Edison conducted
1,000 failed experiments,

00:07:32.664 --> 00:07:33.662
did you know that?

00:07:33.662 --> 00:07:35.090
I didn't know that.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:38.340
Because the 1,001st was the light bulb.

00:07:40.593 --> 00:07:41.676
Fall forward.

00:07:43.088 --> 00:07:47.072
Every failed experiment is
one step closer to success.

00:07:47.072 --> 00:07:49.904
Bruce Lee, you know,
studies many martial arts.

00:07:49.904 --> 00:07:51.632
(muffled speaking)

00:07:51.632 --> 00:07:52.548
(audience laughing)

00:07:52.548 --> 00:07:53.381
(muffled speaking)

00:07:53.381 --> 00:07:54.894
He said, take what's useful.

00:07:54.894 --> 00:07:59.061
So, I've taken from different
styles, and, you know,

00:08:00.075 --> 00:08:03.300
my youngest daughter,
Olivia's an actress now.

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:04.799
She's at NYU.
(audience cheering)

00:08:04.799 --> 00:08:06.238
In fact, she just finished her first movie

00:08:06.238 --> 00:08:07.706
with Lee Daniels, yeah.

00:08:07.706 --> 00:08:08.539
- Oh wow.

00:08:08.539 --> 00:08:09.372
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Nice.

00:08:09.372 --> 00:08:10.812
(audience applauding)

00:08:10.812 --> 00:08:12.256
(muffled speaking)

00:08:12.256 --> 00:08:14.033
- And I tell her that, you know,

00:08:14.033 --> 00:08:18.236
there is no one discipline,
there is no one gospel

00:08:18.236 --> 00:08:19.757
when it comes to acting.

00:08:19.757 --> 00:08:21.299
So I take a little from this,

00:08:21.299 --> 00:08:22.299
take a little from that.

00:08:22.299 --> 00:08:23.132
(audience applauding)

00:08:23.132 --> 00:08:24.760
I had breakfast with
Archbishop Desmond Tutu,

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:26.187
and lunch with Nelson Mandela.

00:08:26.187 --> 00:08:27.020
- [Interviewer] Wow.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:28.882
- And actually, when
he came to the States,

00:08:28.882 --> 00:08:29.881
- [Interviewer] Yeah?

00:08:29.881 --> 00:08:31.506
- The last time he came,
he came to California,

00:08:31.506 --> 00:08:32.339
came to our house.

00:08:32.339 --> 00:08:33.172
(crowd cheering)

00:08:33.172 --> 00:08:34.072
- Well that's pretty good.

00:08:34.072 --> 00:08:35.626
Now, you see that's...
(crowd applauding)

00:08:35.626 --> 00:08:37.678
- I'll tell you a funny story, too.

00:08:37.678 --> 00:08:38.511
(chuckling)

00:08:38.511 --> 00:08:39.605
He'll be mad at me for telling this,

00:08:39.605 --> 00:08:41.247
but I'll tell it anyway.
- Yeah.

00:08:41.247 --> 00:08:42.080
(audience laughing)

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:43.265
- Sylvester Stallone, he lives up in the,

00:08:43.265 --> 00:08:44.390
we're like in a gated community,

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:45.473
he lives over there.

00:08:45.473 --> 00:08:47.186
And he wasn't invited, (laughs)

00:08:47.186 --> 00:08:48.019
(muffled speaking)

00:08:48.019 --> 00:08:49.258
I mean, I would've invited him, but,

00:08:49.258 --> 00:08:50.091
- Yeah.

00:08:50.091 --> 00:08:51.912
- We had about maybe 40, 50 people there,

00:08:51.912 --> 00:08:53.585
you know, and I had my children.

00:08:53.585 --> 00:08:55.443
I said you can invite one friend,

00:08:55.443 --> 00:08:58.346
and, you know, Oprah Winfrey,
different people were there.

00:08:58.346 --> 00:09:00.795
And the security, I had
security helicopters

00:09:00.795 --> 00:09:01.794
like it was the President.

00:09:01.794 --> 00:09:02.627
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:09:02.627 --> 00:09:03.593
- They're like, Mr. Washington,

00:09:03.593 --> 00:09:05.553
Rocky's out front he's pacing.
(audience laughing)

00:09:05.553 --> 00:09:06.995
He's like,

00:09:06.995 --> 00:09:08.988
he's like lookin' in the gates.

00:09:08.988 --> 00:09:10.153
(laughing)

00:09:10.153 --> 00:09:13.531
He's like, should we let him in?

00:09:13.531 --> 00:09:14.817
I'm like, yeah, let Sylvester come in.

00:09:14.817 --> 00:09:15.650
- Yeah, yeah.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:16.730
(audience laughing)

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:18.185
- Denzel Washington.
- He said he was outside

00:09:18.185 --> 00:09:19.162
lookin' sad, like,

00:09:19.162 --> 00:09:19.995
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:09:19.995 --> 00:09:22.280
(Denzel and audience laughing)

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:23.295
- Can you see Rocky?

00:09:23.295 --> 00:09:25.555
(muffled speaking)

00:09:25.555 --> 00:09:26.546
- Mr. Mandela, how you doin'?

00:09:26.546 --> 00:09:27.870
- Mr. Mandela, how you doin'?

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:28.703
- [Interviewer] How you doin'?

00:09:28.703 --> 00:09:29.536
- How you doin'?

00:09:29.536 --> 00:09:30.369
Yo, hey, Duran.

00:09:30.369 --> 00:09:31.557
(audience laughing)

00:09:31.557 --> 00:09:33.209
It's Mandela here, Mandela over here.

00:09:33.209 --> 00:09:35.706
(laughing)

00:09:35.706 --> 00:09:36.891
(clapping)

00:09:36.891 --> 00:09:38.779
(audience applauding)

00:09:38.779 --> 00:09:39.612
(whooshing)

00:09:39.612 --> 00:09:40.445
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:09:40.445 --> 00:09:42.656
I made this video because
Aurene Dark Knight

00:09:42.656 --> 00:09:45.006
asked me to, so if there's
a famous entrepreneur

00:09:45.006 --> 00:09:46.735
that you want me to profile next,

00:09:46.735 --> 00:09:48.303
leave it in the comments below

00:09:48.303 --> 00:09:49.476
and I'll see what I can do.

00:09:49.476 --> 00:09:51.760
I'd also love to know which
of Denzel's top 10 rules

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:54.234
meant the most to you,
had the biggest impact,

00:09:54.234 --> 00:09:56.474
leave it in the comments and
I'll join the discussion.

00:09:56.474 --> 00:09:58.784
Thank you so much for
watching, continue to believe

00:09:58.784 --> 00:10:00.249
and I'll see you soon.

00:10:00.249 --> 00:10:02.582
(whooshing)

00:10:06.659 --> 00:10:09.876
- You will fail at some point
in your life, accept it.

00:10:09.876 --> 00:10:12.352
You will lose, you will
embarrass yourself,

00:10:12.352 --> 00:10:15.010
you will suck at something.
(audience laughing)

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:16.423
There's no doubt about it.

00:10:16.423 --> 00:10:18.954
And I know that's probably
not a traditional message

00:10:18.954 --> 00:10:20.964
for a graduation ceremony, but hey,

00:10:20.964 --> 00:10:23.426
I'm telling you, embrace it,

00:10:23.426 --> 00:10:24.786
because it's inevitable.

00:10:24.786 --> 00:10:26.286
And I should know.

00:10:27.327 --> 00:10:30.149
In the acting business,
you fail all the time.

00:10:30.149 --> 00:10:33.714
Early on in my career,
I auditioned for a part

00:10:33.714 --> 00:10:35.732
in a Broadway musical.

00:10:35.732 --> 00:10:37.891
Perfect role for me, I thought.

00:10:37.891 --> 00:10:39.875
Except for the fact that I can't sing.

00:10:39.875 --> 00:10:41.504
(audience laughing)

00:10:41.504 --> 00:10:44.856
So, I'm in the wings,
I'm about to go on stage,

00:10:44.856 --> 00:10:46.899
but they guy in front of me,

00:10:46.899 --> 00:10:49.232
he's singing like Pavarotti,

00:10:50.548 --> 00:10:53.823
he's just (mimicking operatic music).

00:10:53.823 --> 00:10:55.288
He's just going on and on and on,

00:10:55.288 --> 00:10:58.226
and I'm just shrinking, I'm
getting smaller and smaller.

00:10:58.226 --> 00:11:01.187
So they say oh, thank you very
much, thank you very much,

00:11:01.187 --> 00:11:03.242
and you'll be hearing from us.

00:11:03.242 --> 00:11:06.023
So, I come out with my little sheet music

00:11:06.023 --> 00:11:10.190
and it was Just My Imagination
by the Temptations,

00:11:11.624 --> 00:11:13.145
that's what I came up with.
(audience applauding)

00:11:13.145 --> 00:11:15.734
So, I hand it to the accompanist,

00:11:15.734 --> 00:11:18.358
and she looks at it, and looks at me,

00:11:18.358 --> 00:11:22.306
and looks out at the director
and is like, alright.

00:11:22.306 --> 00:11:23.397
(audience laughing)

00:11:23.397 --> 00:11:25.939
So I start, you know, I'm going to sing.

00:11:25.939 --> 00:11:27.542
I'm like,

00:11:27.542 --> 00:11:31.511
♫ This is my imagination
(audience laughing)

00:11:31.511 --> 00:11:33.529
♫ Once again

00:11:33.529 --> 00:11:37.079
♫ Coming away with me

00:11:37.079 --> 00:11:38.292
And they're not saying anything,

00:11:38.292 --> 00:11:39.987
so I'm thinkin' I'm gettin' better,

00:11:39.987 --> 00:11:41.321
so I just start gettin' into it.

00:11:41.321 --> 00:11:42.598
(audience laughing)

00:11:42.598 --> 00:11:43.598
♫ It was just my imagination

00:11:43.598 --> 00:11:44.615
(humming)

00:11:44.615 --> 00:11:47.615
They said, oh, thank you, thank you.

00:11:49.334 --> 00:11:51.121
Thank you very much, Mr.
Washington, thank you.

00:11:51.121 --> 00:11:53.954
So I assumed I didn't get the job.

00:11:55.555 --> 00:11:56.878
But the next part of the audition,

00:11:56.878 --> 00:11:58.095
he called me back.

00:11:58.095 --> 00:12:00.417
The next part of the
audition is the acting part

00:12:00.417 --> 00:12:01.845
of the audition.

00:12:01.845 --> 00:12:03.632
Now I'm like, hey, okay,
maybe I can't sing,

00:12:03.632 --> 00:12:05.146
but I know I can act.

00:12:05.146 --> 00:12:07.596
So they pair me with this guy, and again,

00:12:07.596 --> 00:12:09.278
I didn't know about musical theater.

00:12:09.278 --> 00:12:11.890
And musical theater is big,
so they can reach everyone

00:12:11.890 --> 00:12:14.620
all the way in the back of the stadium.

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:18.091
And I'm more from a
realistic, naturalistic

00:12:18.091 --> 00:12:20.123
kind of acting where you, you know,

00:12:20.123 --> 00:12:22.538
you actually talk to
the person next to you.

00:12:22.538 --> 00:12:24.799
So, I dunno what my line was,

00:12:24.799 --> 00:12:27.062
my line was, well, hand me the cup.

00:12:27.062 --> 00:12:30.882
And his line was, well, I will
hand you the cup, my dear,

00:12:30.882 --> 00:12:32.476
the cup will be there
(audience laughing)

00:12:32.476 --> 00:12:34.143
to be handed to you.

00:12:38.186 --> 00:12:41.307
I said, okay (laughs)

00:12:41.307 --> 00:12:42.566
(audience laughing)

00:12:42.566 --> 00:12:45.242
Well, should I give you the cup back?

00:12:45.242 --> 00:12:47.054
Oh yes, you should give it back to me

00:12:47.054 --> 00:12:48.667
because you know that is my cup,

00:12:48.667 --> 00:12:50.971
and it should be given back to me.

00:12:50.971 --> 00:12:54.682
(audience laughing)

00:12:54.682 --> 00:12:56.432
I didn't get the job.

00:12:59.527 --> 00:13:01.277
But here's the thing,

00:13:02.116 --> 00:13:03.283
I didn't quit.

00:13:04.914 --> 00:13:06.497
I didn't fall back.

00:13:08.057 --> 00:13:10.505
I walked out of there to
prepare for the next audition,

00:13:10.505 --> 00:13:13.643
and the next audition,
and the next audition.

00:13:13.643 --> 00:13:14.476
I prayed.

00:13:16.664 --> 00:13:17.497
I prayed,

00:13:18.438 --> 00:13:19.521
and I prayed.

00:13:21.702 --> 00:13:25.638
But I continued to fail,
and fail, and fail.

00:13:25.638 --> 00:13:27.677
But it didn't matter,
because you know what?

00:13:27.677 --> 00:13:30.700
There's an old saying, you
hang around the barber shop

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:32.985
long enough, sooner or later
you're going to get a haircut.

00:13:32.985 --> 00:13:35.735
(audience laughing)

00:13:35.735 --> 00:13:38.487
So you will catch a break,
and I did catch a break.

00:13:38.487 --> 00:13:42.654
Last year, I did a play
called Fences on Broadway.

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:46.420
(audience cheering)
(audience applauding)

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:48.661
Someone talked about it.

00:13:48.661 --> 00:13:50.244
Won the Tony award.

00:13:52.026 --> 00:13:53.880
And I didn't have to sing
by the way (chuckles).

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:56.215
(audience laughing)

00:13:56.215 --> 00:13:57.576
But here's the kicker,

00:13:57.576 --> 00:14:00.004
it was at the Court Theater,

00:14:00.004 --> 00:14:02.951
it was at the same theater that I failed

00:14:02.951 --> 00:14:06.053
that first audition 30 years prior.

00:14:06.053 --> 00:14:11.045
(audience applauding)
(audience cheering)

00:14:11.045 --> 00:14:13.971
The point is, and I'll pick up the pace,

00:14:13.971 --> 00:14:16.502
the point is, every graduate here today

00:14:16.502 --> 00:14:20.294
has the training and
the talent to succeed.

00:14:20.294 --> 00:14:23.223
But do you have the guts to fail?

00:14:23.223 --> 00:14:24.373
I'm a father first.

00:14:24.373 --> 00:14:27.077
I don't get life mixed
up with makin' a livin'.

00:14:27.077 --> 00:14:29.203
I was there for all four
of my children bein' born,

00:14:29.203 --> 00:14:30.677
when the first one was born,

00:14:30.677 --> 00:14:32.837
I recognized the difference between life

00:14:32.837 --> 00:14:34.821
and makin' a living.

00:14:34.821 --> 00:14:35.947
They're life, you know?

00:14:35.947 --> 00:14:37.574
Our family is life, you know,

00:14:37.574 --> 00:14:39.013
acting is makin' a living.

00:14:39.013 --> 00:14:40.310
- What do you think the biggest difference

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:41.927
between doing a play and doing, I mean,

00:14:41.927 --> 00:14:43.924
it's a cliched question, but
I'd be interested to know

00:14:43.924 --> 00:14:44.923
your answer.

00:14:44.923 --> 00:14:46.130
- I think a part of what Viola just said

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:47.765
is that you get to dig deeper,

00:14:47.765 --> 00:14:48.963
and you get another day.

00:14:48.963 --> 00:14:49.968
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:14:49.968 --> 00:14:50.969
- And you get the energy.

00:14:50.969 --> 00:14:53.236
You do a movie and it's 200
people that are used to it,

00:14:53.236 --> 00:14:54.570
and they don't care.

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:55.878
And everybody's doin' their job,

00:14:55.878 --> 00:14:57.564
and it comes into theaters somewhere,

00:14:57.564 --> 00:14:59.482
and you're, you know,
picking your nose at home

00:14:59.482 --> 00:15:00.757
somewhere, clipping your nails.

00:15:00.757 --> 00:15:01.757
- Right.
(audience laughing)

00:15:01.757 --> 00:15:02.590
- There's no energy.

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:03.423
- Right.

00:15:03.423 --> 00:15:05.571
- When I was on Broadway
five years ago, Julius Cesar,

00:15:05.571 --> 00:15:08.126
I made a decision to sign
autographs every night.

00:15:08.126 --> 00:15:09.670
Oh, what I did.
(audience applauding)

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:10.598
(laughing)

00:15:10.598 --> 00:15:13.348
It ain't happening this time, no.

00:15:14.924 --> 00:15:17.671
Only as I rub my knees, only
because my knees are bad,

00:15:17.671 --> 00:15:19.023
it's hard for me to stand.

00:15:19.023 --> 00:15:20.032
But,

00:15:20.032 --> 00:15:21.063
- And why did you decide to do that?

00:15:21.063 --> 00:15:22.773
- Well, you know, I
wanted to say thank you.

00:15:22.773 --> 00:15:23.606
- [Interviewer] Uh-huh.

00:15:23.606 --> 00:15:25.025
- It was my way of saying thank you.

00:15:25.025 --> 00:15:26.128
- [Audience Member] Yes.

00:15:26.128 --> 00:15:29.228
- And the first night, it was
like 5,000 people out there,

00:15:29.228 --> 00:15:30.061
so okay, wait a minute.

00:15:30.061 --> 00:15:32.154
Everybody who has
playbills for my show, so,

00:15:32.154 --> 00:15:34.208
(hosts and audience laughing)

00:15:34.208 --> 00:15:35.759
so yeah, 'cause it was like,
people would just wandering

00:15:35.759 --> 00:15:36.902
up the block.

00:15:36.902 --> 00:15:38.778
Hang on wait a minute, I
forgot, I'm on, you know,

00:15:38.778 --> 00:15:40.441
42nd street and Time Square.

00:15:40.441 --> 00:15:41.948
(hosts and audience laughing)

00:15:41.948 --> 00:15:43.462
But honestly,

00:15:43.462 --> 00:15:44.490
- [Interviewer] You
were signing for weeks.

00:15:44.490 --> 00:15:46.638
- The energy, I was out there
hour and a half every night,

00:15:46.638 --> 00:15:47.471
every show,

00:15:47.471 --> 00:15:48.304
- [Interviewer] Wow.

00:15:48.304 --> 00:15:49.870
- But the energy you
got back, the stories,

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:53.586
the five little old ladies who
just drove in from Detroit,

00:15:53.586 --> 00:15:55.408
and I'm like well, what
you all doing tonight?

00:15:55.408 --> 00:15:56.720
They say, we gettin' back in the car baby,

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:58.253
we got to go back tonight.
(audience laughing)

00:15:58.253 --> 00:16:00.285
And you know, a little 84 year old lady

00:16:00.285 --> 00:16:02.734
was like, baby, if I
was three years younger.

00:16:02.734 --> 00:16:04.984
(laughing)

00:16:07.082 --> 00:16:08.084
True story.

00:16:08.084 --> 00:16:09.816
True story.

00:16:09.816 --> 00:16:13.160
Then we had, and I say this also,

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:15.562
'cause I hope it happens with this play,

00:16:15.562 --> 00:16:18.790
we had some high schools
come, we had one school

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:20.277
from somewhere, I forgot where,

00:16:20.277 --> 00:16:22.471
and they were doing Julius Cesar,

00:16:22.471 --> 00:16:23.493
and some young kid was like,

00:16:23.493 --> 00:16:24.326
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:16:24.326 --> 00:16:26.638
- Well, you know, my Brutus
is a bit different, you know.

00:16:26.638 --> 00:16:27.614
(hosts and audience laughing)

00:16:27.614 --> 00:16:28.868
He's more of a classic stoic.

00:16:28.868 --> 00:16:31.759
But it was great to engage in the exchange

00:16:31.759 --> 00:16:35.936
with these young people,
there was an energy.

00:16:35.936 --> 00:16:38.801
I got more from it than
I gave to the people

00:16:38.801 --> 00:16:40.741
that I signed for, that's, you know,

00:16:40.741 --> 00:16:42.900
you don't get that.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.679
You know, the odd thing
about success in film

00:16:45.679 --> 00:16:48.197
is the budget's get bigger,
you make more money,

00:16:48.197 --> 00:16:50.149
but they become more formulaic.

00:16:50.149 --> 00:16:50.982
- [Interviewer] Mmm, yes.

00:16:50.982 --> 00:16:51.891
- So you don't get the opportunity,

00:16:51.891 --> 00:16:54.178
so I, God willing, if I
stay around long enough,

00:16:54.178 --> 00:16:56.217
this is what I'm going to be doin'.

